<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yavne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yibna, Jamnia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yavne is a town on the coastal plain of Israel, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, known in antiquity as Jamnia. After the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> in 70 CE it became a centre of rabbinic learning. As Yibna, it was the site of the crusader castle of Ibelin, built by King Fulk in 1141, which gave its name to the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">Ibelin family</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yavne.jpg" alt="Yavne" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Municipality of Yavne · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yavne_panoramic_view2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yavne lies in the southern coastal plain, about six kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a> and on the eastern border of the Yavne sand dunes. The ancient town stood on a natural kurkar hill, now the archaeological site of Tel Yavne, which excavations show to have been inhabited continuously from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> until the British Mandate. The name appears in many English translations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> as Jabneh, in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources as Jamnia, and in the Ottoman and British periods as Yibna. The ancient town had a long history of wine production.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times King Herod bequeathed Iamnia to his sister Salome, and it then passed to Emperor Augustus, who ran it as a private imperial estate. In 68 CE, after Vespasian had put down the revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, his army took the town and garrisoned it. According to rabbinic tradition, Rabbi Yohanan ben Zakkai obtained Vespasian's permission to settle there and teach his disciples, and after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> his school functioned as a Sanhedrin. A supposed Council of Jamnia that fixed the Jewish biblical canon was long theorised, but current scholarship largely rejects it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> called the town Ibelin and built a castle there in 1141. Its namesake family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">House of Ibelin</a>, was important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> and later in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Saladin sacked it before his army was routed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montgisard">Battle of Montgisard</a> in 1177, and in August 1187 it was retaken and burnt. The parish church became a mosque, and a minaret was added in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> period in 1337. During the 1948 war most of the villagers of Yibna fled, and the Israeli troops that arrived on 5 June found the village almost deserted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Modern Yavne was founded in October 1948 as a transit camp for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Arab countries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the first neighbourhood followed in early 1949. It received city status in 1986 and lies in the Central District of Israel. The minaret of the old mosque still stands, and excavations at Tel Yavne since 2005 have uncovered the gate room of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> castle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yavne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Council of <b>Jamnia</b>, once credited with fixing it, is not believed by modern scholars to have been an authoritative council.</blockquote><a href="/work/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1141 King Fulk built a castle at <b>Yibna</b> (Yavne), on the coastal plain south of Jaffa, as one of a ring of forts to contain the Fatimid garrison of Ascalon.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ibelin">Lordship of Ibelin</a></li></ul></section>
